ther line through (3,2) and (4,1) slope (2-1)/(3-4)=1/(-1)=-1, not allowed. So maybe we need to use vertical line for column 5? But we already used vertical line for column 1. We have only one non-sunny line. So we cannot use vertical line for column 5. So we need to cover (5,1) with a sunny line. That's okay.\n\nBut we need to cover all points. Let's try to systematically see if we can cover all points with 4 sunny lines. This is a combinatorial geometry problem. Might be possible. Let's try to find a set of 4 sunny lines that cover all points. Let's denote lines L1, L2, L3, L4. Each line can intersect each column at most once. So each column can have at most 4 points covered by sunny lines (since we have 4 lines). Column 2 has 4 points, so each point must be covered by a distinct sunny line (i.e., each of the 4 lines must intersect column 2 at a distinct point). So each line must pass through exactly one of the points in column 2. So we can assign: L1 passes through (2,1), L2 through (2,2), L3 through (2,3), L4 through (2,4). Then each line also must cover points in other columns. For each line, we can try to also cover a point in column 3, column 4, column 5. Since each line can intersect each column at most once, we can try to assign each line to also intersect column 3 at some point, etc. Let's see if we can assign such that all points in columns 3-5 are covered.\n\nColumn 3 has points (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). We have 4 lines, each can intersect column 3 at most once. So we need to cover 3 points, we can assign them to any 3 of the lines. Similarly column 4 has points (4,1),(4,2). Column 5 has point (5,1). So we need to assign each point to a line that also passes through its assigned column 2 point.\n\nThus we need to find lines that pass through a given point in column 2 and a given point in column 3 (or 4,5). For each line, we can choose which column(s) it will intersect besides column 2. Since we have 4 lines, we can assign them as follows:\n\n- L1: passes through (2,1) and (3,1) (vertical? No, same x coordinate? Actually (2,1) and (3,1) have same y coordinate, horizontal line y=1, not allowed. So cannot assign L1 to (3,1). Could assign L1 to (3,2) maybe? (2,1) and (3,2) slope (1-2)/(2-3) = -1/(-1)=1, allowed. So L1: (2,1)-(3,2). That line also passes through (4,3)? Let's check: slope 1, line equation y - 1 = 1*(x-2) => y = x - 1. At x=4, y=3, which is not a point in T_5 (since (4,3) has sum 7 >6). So not needed. At x=5, y=4, not in T_5. So L1 covers (2,1) and (3,2). Good.\n\n- L2: passes through (2,2) and maybe (3,3) slope (2-3)/(2-3) = -1/(-1)=1, allowed. So L2: (2,2)-(3,3). Equation y - 2 = 1*(x-2) => y = x. At x=4, y=4 (not in T_5 because (4,4) sum 8 >6). So L2 covers (2,2) and (3,3).\n\n- L3: passes through (2,3) and maybe (4,1) slope (3-1)/(2-4)=2/(-2)=-1, not allowed. Could pass through (4,2) slope (3-2)/(2-4)=1/(-2)=-1/2, allowed. So L3: (2,3)-(4,2). Equation: slope -1/2, line: y - 3 = -1/2 (x-2) => y = -1/2 x + 4. At x=3, y = -1.5 + 4 = 2.5, not integer. So L3 covers (2,3) and (4,2). Good.\n\n- L4: passes through (2,4) and maybe (5,1) slope (4-1)/(2-5)=3/(-3)=-1, not allowed. Could pass through (4,1) slope (4-1)/(2-4)=3/(-2)=-3/2, allowed. So L4: (2,4)-(4,1). Equation: slope -3/2, line: y - 4 = -3/2 (x-2) => y = -3/2 x + 7. At x=5, y = -7.5 + 7 = -0.5, not integer. So L4 covers (2,4) and (4,1). Good.\n\nNow we have covered column 2 points all covered. Column 3 points: (3,1) not covered yet; (3,2) covered by L1; (3,3) covered by L2. So (3,1) remains uncovered. Column 4 points: (4,1) covered by L4; (4,2) covered by L3. Column 5 point: (5,1) uncovered. So we have uncovered (3,1) and (5,1). We have used all 4 sunny lines; we cannot add more. Could we adjust assignments to cover (3,1) and (5,1) instead? Let's try to reassign lines.\n\nWe need to cover (5,1) with a sunny line. Which column 2 point can be paired with (5,1) to form a sunny line? Let's compute slopes:\n\n- Pair (2,1) with (5,1): slope 0 (horizontal) not allowed.\n- Pair (2,2) with (5,1): slope (2-1)/(2-5)=1/(-3) = -1/3, allowed. So line through (2,2) and (5,1) is allowed. That line also passes through (3,? ) maybe (3, 4/3?) not integer. So we could assign L2 to (2,2)-(5,1) instead of (2,2)-(3,3). Then (3,3) would be uncovered. But maybe another line can cover (3,3). Let's see.\n\nWe need to cover (3,1) as well. Which column 2 point can pair with (3,1) to form a sunny line? Pair (2,1)-(3,1) horizontal not allowed. Pair (2,2)-(3,1) slope (2-1)/(2-3)=1/(-1)=-1, not allowed. Pair (2,3)-(3,1) slope (3-1)/(2-3)=2/(-1)=-2, allowed. So line through (2,3)-(3,1) is allowed. That line also passes through (4,? ) maybe (4,-1) not in T_5. So we could assign L3 to (2,3)-(3,1). Then (3,1) covered.\n\nNow we need to cover (3,3) and (4,2) and (4,1) maybe. Let's see.\n\nWe have L1 currently (2,1)-(3,2) covering (3,2). L2 (2,2)-(5,1) covering (5,1). L3 (2,3)-(3,1) covering (3,1). L4 (2,4)-(4,1) covering (4,1). So far covered: column 2 all points (2,1)-(2,4). Column 3: (3,2) covered by L1; (3,1) covered by L3; (3,3) not covered. Column 4: (4,1) covered by L4; (4,2) not covered. Column 5: (5,1) covered by L2. So uncovered: (3,3) and (4,2). Could we adjust L1 to also cover (4,2)? L1 currently (2,1)-(3,2). Could we change L1 to (2,1)-(4,2)? Slope (1-2)/(2-4) = -1/(-2)=1/2, allowed. That line passes through (3,? ) maybe (3,1.5) not integer. So L1 would cover (2,1) and (4,2). Then (3,2) uncovered. Could we assign L4 to cover (3,2) maybe? L4 currently (2,4)-(4,1). Could we change L4 to (2,4)-(3,2)? Slope (4-2)/(2-3)=2/(-1)=-2, allowed. That line passes through (4,? ) maybe (4,0) not in T_5. So L4 would cover (2,4) and (3,2). Then (4,1) uncovered. Could we assign L2 to cover (4,1) maybe? L2 currently (2,2)-(5,1). Could we change L2 to (2,2)-(4,1)? Slope (2-1)/(2-4)=1/(-2)=-1/2, allowed. That line passes through (3,? ) maybe (3,1.5) not integer. So L2 would cover (2,2) and (4,1). Then (5,1) uncovered. Could we assign L3 to cover (5,1) maybe? L3 currently (2,3)-(3,1). Could we change L3 to (2,3)-(5,1)? Slope (3-1)/(2-5)=2/(-3)=-2/3, allowed. That line passes through (3,? ) maybe (3, 5/3?) not integer. So L3 would cover (2,3) and (5,1). Then (3,1) uncovered. So we need to adjust again.\n\nIt seems possible to find a configuration, but it's not trivial. However, the solution claims k≥4 impossible. Let's test n=5 with k=4 using a systematic approach: we need to cover all points with 5 lines, 4 sunny, 1 non-sunny. The non-sunny line could be vertical covering a column, or horizontal covering many points, or slope -1 covering many points. Let's try to find a configuration manually or reason about impossibility.\n\nWe need to cover column 1 (size 5) and column 2 (size 4) etc. Since we have only one non-sunny line, we cannot cover both column 1 and column 2 fully with vertical lines. So we need to use sunny lines to cover many points in those columns. But each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So with 4 sunny lines, we can cover at most 4 points in column 1 and at most 4 points in column 2. Column 1 has 5 points, column 2 has 4 points. So we need the non-sunny line to cover at least one point in column 1 (maybe the remaining point). But we also need to cover all points in column 1. Since we have 4 sunny lines, they can cover at most 4 points in column 1. So we need the non-sunny line to cover the remaining point in column 1. That non-sunny line could be vertical x=1 covering all 5 points in column 1, which would also cover all points in column 1, leaving the sunny lines to cover the rest. That seems plausible: use vertical line x=1 (non-sunny) covering column 1. Then we have 4 sunny lines to cover columns 2-5. Column 2 has 4 points, which can be covered by the 4 sunny lines each covering one point. Column 3 has 3 points, we need to assign them to some of the sunny lines (maybe 3 of them). Column 4 has 2 points, assign to 2 lines. Column 5 has 1 point, assign to 1 line. Since each line can intersect