e claim \"forcing a vertical line\" is correct: we need at least one vertical line among the n lines. So we have at least one vertical line.\n\nNow they say \"Then analyzing columns x=n-1 and x=n shows that point (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with points in column n-1 (any such line is horizontal or slope -1, both non-sunny). This forces a third sunny line, contradicting k=2.\" Let's examine that.\n\nAfter we have a vertical line at x=n-2, we have n-1 remaining lines to cover the rest. Among those, we have exactly 2 sunny lines total (k=2). So we have at most 2 sunny lines total, one of which could be the vertical line? No, vertical line is non-sunny. So we have 2 sunny lines among the remaining n-1 lines.\n\nNow consider column n-1: points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Column n: point (n,1). We need to cover these three points. We have at most 2 sunny lines left, plus possibly other non-sunny lines (vertical/horizontal/diag). But we already used one vertical line at x=n-2. We could also use a horizontal line y=1 (non-sunny) to cover (n-1,1) and (n,1). That would leave (n-1,2) uncovered. Could we cover (n-1,2) with a sunny line? Yes, we could use a sunny line passing through (n-1,2) and some earlier point. That would be okay. So we would have used one sunny line for (n-1,2). So we would have used only one sunny line for these columns. So why does the solution claim that (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with points in column n-1? Let's parse: \"point (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with points in column n-1 (any such line is horizontal or slope -1, both non-sunny).\" Indeed, if we try to cover (n,1) and (n-1,2) with a line, slope = (2-1)/(n-1-n) = 1/(-1) = -1, which is not sunny. If we try to cover (n,1) and (n-1,1) with a line, slope = (1-1)/(n-1-n) = 0, horizontal, not sunny. So any line that passes through (n,1) and any point in column n-1 is either horizontal (if same y) or slope -1 (if y differs by 1). So indeed, a sunn